<!-- this exercise was developed by Spencer Bagley as part of the PROTEUS grant-->

<setup>
    <function name="f">-0.25*x^3+1.5*x+1</function>
    <derivative name="fprime">$f</derivative>
    <derivative name="fPP">$fprime</derivative>
    <point name="P" labelIsName labelPosition="lowerRight">
        <constrainTo>$f</constrainTo>
    </point>
    <point 
        name="sd" 
        labelPosition="lowerRight"
        styleNumber="4"> 
        ($P.x, $$fPP($P.x))
        <label>2nd der.</label>
    </point>
    <number name="m">$$fprime($P.x)</number>
    <point name="left">
        ($P.x-1/sqrt(1+$m^2), $P.y-$m/sqrt(1+$m^2))
    </point>
    <point name="right">
        ($P.x+1/sqrt(1+$m^2), $P.y+$m/sqrt(1+$m^2))
    </point>
    <lineSegment name="hi" stylenumber="2" endpoints="$left $right"/>
</setup>
<p>
    On the following graph of the function <m>f</m>, you can drag the point <m>P</m> along the graph of <m>f</m>.  As you do so, you will also see a a purple diamond point whose height represents the value of the second derivative of <m>f</m> at the same <m>x</m>-value as point <m>P</m>.  As you move the point <m>P</m>, note that the tangent line to <m>f</m> moves along with the point; remember that the second derivative of <m>f</m> measures how the slope of the tangent line to <m>f</m> changes as <m>x</m> changes.  
</p>
<graph
    fixAxes="true" showNavigation="false"
    xmin="-3" xmax="3"
    ymin="-3" ymax="3" >
  <shortDescription>
    graph of a cubic function f, its tangent line at a movable point, and the value of the second derivative at that point
  </shortDescription>
    $f
    $P
    $sd
    $hi
</graph>
<p>
    When the purple diamond point is <alert>above</alert> the horizontal axis,
    the second derivative of <m>f</m> is <!-- positive -->
    <answer>
        <choiceInput inline>
          <shortDescription>sign options</shortDescription>
            <choice credit = "0"><math>?</math></choice>
            <choice credit = "1">positive</choice>
            <choice credit = "0">negative</choice>
        </choiceInput> 
    </answer>, the tangent line near <m>P</m> is <!-- below -->
    <answer>
        <choiceInput inline>
          <shortDescription>above or below options</shortDescription>
            <choice credit = "0"><math>?</math></choice>
            <choice credit = "0">above</choice>
            <choice credit = "1">below</choice>
        </choiceInput>
        </answer>
    the graph of <m>f</m>, 
    and the graph of <m>f</m> opens <!-- upward -->
    <answer>
        <choiceInput inline>
          <shortDescription>upward or downward options</shortDescription>
            <choice credit = "0"><math>?</math></choice>
            <choice credit = "1">upward</choice>
            <choice credit = "0">downward</choice>
        </choiceInput>
    </answer>.
</p>
<p>
    When the purple diamond point <alert>below</alert> the horizontal axis,
    the second derivative of <m>f</m> is <!-- negative -->
    <answer>
        <choiceInput inline>
          <shortDescription>sign options</shortDescription>
            <choice credit = "0"><math>?</math></choice>
            <choice credit = "0">positive</choice>
            <choice credit = "1">negative</choice>
        </choiceInput>
    </answer>
    below the horizontal axis, 
    the tangent line near <m>P</m> is <!-- above -->
    <answer>
        <choiceInput inline>
          <shortDescription>sign options</shortDescription>
            <choice credit = "0"><math>?</math></choice>
            <choice credit = "1">above</choice>
            <choice credit = "0">below</choice>
        </choiceInput>
    </answer>
    the graph of <m>f</m>, and the graph of <m>f</m> opens <!-- downward-->
    <answer>
        <choiceInput inline>
          <shortDescription>upward or downward options</shortDescription>
            <choice credit = "0"><math>?</math></choice>
            <choice credit = "0">upward</choice>
            <choice credit = "1">downward</choice>
        </choiceInput>
    </answer>.
</p>